var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"Tree reading, writing and manipulation (readnewick, readnexus, mrca, ladderize!, tips, …) come from PhyloIO.jl and are re-exported.","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Figure","page":"API","text":"","title":"Figure"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Layers","page":"API","text":"","title":"Layers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Output","page":"API","text":"","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Makie","page":"API","text":"","title":"Makie"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Fonts","page":"API","text":"","title":"Fonts"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Colours","page":"API","text":"","title":"Colours"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree","page":"API","text":"PlotPhyloTree\n\nPublication-quality phylogenetic tree figures in pure Julia, drawn as SVG (PNG/PDF with the Librsvg extension). Rectangular, circular and fan layouts, clade highlights and labels, support symbols, ancestral-state pies, node age bars, time scales with geological colours, heatmap and bar rings, and images.\n\nusing PlotPhyloTree\ntree = randtree(40)\np = treeplot(tree; layout=:circular)\nhighlight!(p, tipnames(tree)[1:8]; label=\"Clade A\")\nsavefig(\"tree.svg\", p)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.treeplot","page":"API","text":"treeplot(tree; kwargs...) -> TreePlot\n\nStart a tree figure. tree is a PhyloTree, a Newick string, or a path to a Newick/Nexus file.\n\nLayout\n\nlayout = :rectangular — or :circular, :fan (an open circle), or :dendrogram (rectangular, growing downwards).\nbranchstyle = :square — elbows; :round (rounded corners), :curved (smooth sweeps) or :slanted (straight lines from parent to child).\ncladogram = false — ignore branch lengths and align the tips.\nladderize = false — sort clades by size (true, or :left/:right).\ndirection = :right — rectangular only: :right, :left, :down, :up.\nopenangle — circular/fan: gap in degrees (default 6 for :circular, 180 for :fan). rotation = 0 — rotate the whole circle clockwise (degrees).\ninner = 0 — circular: radius (px) of an empty hole at the centre.\nrootedge = 0 — length in px of a stem drawn below the root.\nradius, width, tipspacing — size of the tree itself in px (the circle's radius to the furthest tip, or the depth and per-tip spacing of a rectangular tree). All are chosen from the number of tips by default.\n\nBranches\n\ncolor — a colour; a vector/dictionary/function of per-node values; or a Symbol naming a node annotation (e.g. :rate). Numbers are coloured with colormap over colorrange and get a colour bar; categories get palette colours and a legend.\nlinewidth — a number or per-node values; linestyle = :solid.\n\nText and theme\n\ntiplabels = true, italic = false, align = false, fontsize (auto).\nfont = \"Helvetica Neue, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif\" — the font list written into SVG output.\nmetrics — the font whose widths the layout uses to size labels: a FontMetrics from fontmetrics, or :helvetica, :lmroman, :lmsans (Latin Modern: LaTeX's default) or :times. By default it follows font. Match it to the document font when exporting to TikZ or CeTZ, where labels are set in the document's font.\ntheme = :light — or :dark, :minimal (transparent background).\nbackground — overrides the theme's background colour.\ntitle, subtitle, legend = :auto (:right, :bottom, :topright, :topleft, :bottomright, :bottomleft, :none), legendtitle, margin = 16.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.treeplot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.nodevalues","page":"API","text":"nodevalues(p, v) -> Vector{Any}\n\nExpand v to one value per node (nothing where missing). v may be a dictionary keyed by node references (labels, indices or collections of tip labels meaning their MRCA), a vector over all nodes, a vector over tips in drawing order, a Symbol naming a node annotation, a function of the node index, or a single value for every node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.nodevalues"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.TreePlot","page":"API","text":"TreePlot\n\nA tree figure under construction: create it with treeplot, add layers with the ! functions (highlight!, support!, nodepies!, …), then display it or savefig it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.TreePlot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.tiplabels!","page":"API","text":"tiplabels!(p; text=nothing, italic=false, bold=false, align=false, offset=4,\n           color=nothing, fontsize=nothing, underscores=false, leader=align,\n           colormap=:viridis, palette=:default)\n\nSet the tip labels (replacing the default ones). text changes what is written (a dictionary, vector, function of the node index, or annotation key); by default the node labels, with underscores shown as spaces unless underscores = true. align = true lines the labels up at the outermost tip with dotted leader lines. color may be a colour or per-tip values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.tiplabels!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.notiplabels!","page":"API","text":"notiplabels!(p)  remove the tip labels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.notiplabels!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.highlight!","page":"API","text":"highlight!(p, clade; color=auto, alpha=0.2, label=nothing, from=:stem, to=:labels,\n           stroke=nothing, linewidth=1, branches=false, kwargs...)\nhighlight!(p, clades::AbstractDict; palette=:default, kwargs...)\n\nShade the region behind clade (a node, tip label or collection of tip labels whose MRCA is used), as a wedge in circular layouts and a rounded box in rectangular ones.\n\ncolor — the colour, drawn at opacity alpha; by default the next colour of the palette.\nlabel — also add a cladelabel! in the same colour (extra keywords such as level, fontsize and along go to it).\nfrom — where the shading starts: :stem (half-way along the branch above the clade), :node, :root, or a distance in px before the node.\nto — where it ends: :labels (past the tip labels and its own clade label, or every label if it has none), :tips, :all (past every ring), or a distance in px beyond the furthest tip.\nbranches = true also colours the clade's branches and tip labels.\n\nThe dictionary form highlights several clades, label => clade.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.highlight!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.cladelabel!","page":"API","text":"cladelabel!(p, clade, text; color=:fg, textcolor=nothing, level=1, fontsize=auto,\n            bold=true, italic=false, bar=true, barwidth=3, along=auto, gap=6)\n\nLabel a clade with a bar spanning its tips and a name beyond the tips (and beyond the tip labels). Labels with the same level share a ring; higher levels sit further out, as for nested taxonomic ranks. In circular layouts the name follows the arc (along = true); set along = false for radial text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.cladelabel!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.colorclade!","page":"API","text":"colorclade!(p, clade, color; linewidth=nothing, label=nothing, tiplabels=true)\n\nColour the branches (and, with tiplabels = true, the tip labels) of clade — a node, tip label or collection of tip labels whose MRCA is used. With label, add a legend entry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.colorclade!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.colorbranches!","page":"API","text":"colorbranches!(p, values; colormap=:viridis, colorrange=nothing, palette=:default, title=\"\")\n\nColour every branch by a per-node value (see nodevalues); numbers use colormap and add a colour bar, categories use palette and add a legend, colours are used as given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.colorbranches!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.nodepoints!","page":"API","text":"nodepoints!(p; nodes=:internal, color=:fg, marker=:circle, size=5, strokecolor=:white,\n            strokewidth=0.8, colormap=:viridis, colorrange=nothing, palette=:default,\n            title=\"\", offset=0)\n\nDraw a marker at nodes (:internal, :tips, :all or a list of node references). color, marker and size may be single values or per-node values (see nodevalues); nodes whose colour value is missing are skipped. Numeric colours add a colour bar and categories a legend titled title. Markers: circle, square, diamond, utriangle, dtriangle, triangle, star, cross, xcross, hexagon, pentagon.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.nodepoints!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.tippoints!","page":"API","text":"tippoints!(p; color=:fg, marker=:circle, size=6, kwargs...)\n\nMarkers at the tips, as nodepoints! with nodes = :tips. The tip labels move outwards to make room.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.tippoints!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.support!","page":"API","text":"support!(p; key=:support, breaks=auto, colors=green→red, marker=:circle, size=5,\n         labels=auto, title=\"Support\", hidebelow=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nMark internal nodes by support value (bootstrap, posterior, …) read from the node annotation key (or given as per-node values). Values are binned by breaks (default [70, 90, 95], or [0.7, 0.9, 0.95] for values in 0–1) and each bin gets a colour from colors (highest first) and a legend entry. Nodes below hidebelow are not marked.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.support!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.nodelabels!","page":"API","text":"nodelabels!(p; text=:support, nodes=:internal, fontsize=auto, color=:muted,\n            position=:branch, digits=2, bold=false, halo=true)\n\nWrite text at nodes: an annotation key (default the support values), node labels (text = :label), or per-node values. position = :branch puts it above the branch just before the node (the usual place for support values); :node puts it just after the node. A label that would overlap something else moves to the other place (or below the branch); text too long for its branch always goes after the node. With hideoverlaps = true, labels that fit nowhere are left out instead of drawn over other things.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.nodelabels!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.nodepies!","page":"API","text":"nodepies!(p, data; colors=palette, labels=nothing, radius=7, title=\"\",\n          stroke=:white, strokewidth=0.75)\n\nPie charts at nodes, e.g. ancestral state probabilities. data is a dictionary node => values, an annotation key holding vectors, or a matrix with one row per node of nodes (keyword). labels names the categories for the legend. radius may be a number or per-node values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.nodepies!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.nodebars!","page":"API","text":"nodebars!(p; key=Symbol(\"height_95%_HPD\"), color=(:steelblue, 0.45), width=5, nodes=:internal)\n\nBars across nodes showing an interval of node ages (time before the tips), such as the 95% HPD of node heights in BEAST trees. key may also be per-node (lo, hi) values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.nodebars!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.timescale!","page":"API","text":"timescale!(p; ticks=auto, title=\"\", rings=true, bands=true, labels=true, axis=true,\n           geo=nothing, geoalpha=0.35, origin=0, color=nothing, bandcolor=nothing,\n           fontsize=auto, to=:tips)\n\nA time axis for a dated (ultrametric) tree, measured back from the tips, which are at age origin. Circular layouts get concentric rings and an axis along the opening; rectangular ones get vertical grid lines and an axis below the tree. bands = true shades every other interval.\n\ngeo = :epochs, :periods or :eras colours the background by the geological time scale (ICS colours; ages in Ma), or pass a vector of (name, abbreviation, from, to, colour) tuples. to = :labels or :all carries the rings past the tip labels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.timescale!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.scalebar!","page":"API","text":"scalebar!(p; length=auto, label=auto, position=:bottomleft, linewidth=1.5)\n\nA scale bar for branch lengths, drawn under the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.scalebar!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.tipheatmap!","page":"API","text":"tipheatmap!(p, data; columns=nothing, colormap=:viridis, colorrange=nothing,\n            palette=:default, cellwidth=12, gap=1.5, colnames=true, title=\"\",\n            border=:background, offset=6)\n\nA ring (circular) or block (rectangular) of coloured cells beside the tips. data can be\n\na dictionary tip => value (one column) or tip => vector (several columns),\na matrix with one row per tip, with rows = tip labels (default: the tips in drawing order),\na vector of per-tip values, or\na NamedTuple of any of the one-column forms, giving named columns that are coloured independently (e.g. a numeric trait and a category).\n\nNumbers are coloured by colormap, anything else by palette; each adds a legend titled title (or the column name).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.tipheatmap!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.tipbars!","page":"API","text":"tipbars!(p, values; color=1, length=60, width=0.7, limit=auto, offset=6, title=\"\",\n         colormap=:viridis, palette=:default)\n\nA bar chart of per-tip numbers beside the tips. color is a colour or per-tip values (coloured as in nodepoints!). Bars are scaled so limit (default: the largest value) spans length px; width is the fraction of the tip spacing each bar fills.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.tipbars!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.tipimages!","page":"API","text":"tipimages!(p, images; size=40, offset=8)\n\nImages beside tips or clades. images maps node references (a tip label, or a collection of tip labels meaning their clade, centred on it) to an image: a file path (PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP or SVG), an SVG string, a data URI or a URL. Each image is fitted in a size × size px box.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.tipimages!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.legend!","page":"API","text":"legend!(p, title, entries; kind=:patch, marker=:circle)\n\nAdd a legend section by hand. entries is a vector of label => colour pairs; kind is :patch, :marker or :line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.legend!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.savefig","page":"API","text":"savefig(path, p; scale, kwargs...) -> path\n\nWrite p to path, choosing the format from the extension:\n\n.svg, or .html (an HTML page holding the SVG);\n.png, .pdf — need using Librsvg_jll (an optional extension); scale = 2 multiplies the pixel size of PNG output;\n.tex — a standalone LaTeX document drawing the figure with TikZ, and .tikz — just the tikzpicture, to \\input (see tikzstring);\n.typ — a standalone Typst document drawing it with CeTZ (see cetzstring); pass standalone = false for a file to #include.\n\nFor TikZ and CeTZ, scale = 1 multiplies every length and other keywords go to tikzstring/cetzstring. Their images are written next to path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.savefig"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.svgstring","page":"API","text":"svgstring(p) -> String\n\nThe figure as an SVG document.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.svgstring"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.tikzstring","page":"API","text":"tikzstring(p; standalone=false, scale=1, font=\"\", curvedtext=:native) -> String\n\nThe figure as TikZ code: a tikzpicture to \\input into a LaTeX document, or with standalone = true a complete document that compiles on its own.\n\nGeometry (branches, wedges, rings, symbols) is fixed by the layout, in bp (1 px = 1 bp; scale multiplies every length, font sizes included). Text is handed to LaTeX: labels are typeset in the document's font at the layout's sizes, so changing the document font changes the tree's labels. Ways to pick the font, from widest to narrowest scope:\n\nthe picture uses the font current where it is \\input (e.g. wrap it in {\\sffamily …});\nevery label starts with \\PlotPhyloTreeFont (empty unless you define it, e.g. \\newcommand\\PlotPhyloTreeFont{\\sffamily} in the preamble);\nfont adds LaTeX font code to this figure's labels.\n\nLay the tree out with the metrics of that font (treeplot(…; metrics=:lmroman) for LaTeX's default, or fontmetrics for any font file) so label bands and the legend fit the text LaTeX sets. Curved clade labels use TikZ's text along path, spaced with the document font's own widths; curvedtext = :glyphs places each letter where the layout put it instead.\n\nNeeds \\usepackage{tikz} and \\usetikzlibrary{decorations.text} (the standalone document loads both, plus a font matching the layout metrics). Images are written beside the file by savefig (\"tree.tex\" → tree-img-1.png …); SVG images are converted to PDF, which needs the Librsvg extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.tikzstring"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.cetzstring","page":"API","text":"cetzstring(p; standalone=false, scale=1, cetz=\"0.4.2\", font=nothing,\n           curvedtext=:native) -> String\n\nThe figure as Typst code drawing it with CeTZ. The code imports @preview/cetz:<cetz> and draws a cetz.canvas; #include the saved file in a Typst document, or with standalone = true it sets the page to fit the figure so it compiles on its own (typst compile tree.typ).\n\nGeometry is fixed by the layout (1 px = 1 pt; scale multiplies every length, font sizes included). Text is handed to Typst: labels use the document's font — whatever #set text(font: …) is in force where the file is included — at the layout's sizes. font (a name or list) sets a font for this figure instead; a standalone document picks one matching the layout metrics. Lay the tree out with the metrics of the document font (treeplot(…; metrics=…), see fontmetrics) so label bands and the legend fit. Curved clade labels are spaced by Typst, which measures each letter in the document font (curvedtext = :glyphs places the letters as the layout did instead).\n\nPick cetz to match your Typst version (CeTZ 0.3.0 needs Typst ≥ 0.11, 0.4 needs 0.13.1, 0.5 needs 0.14).\n\nImages are written beside the file by savefig (\"tree.typ\" → tree-img-1.png …); Typst draws SVG images itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.cetzstring"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.preview","page":"API","text":"preview(p) -> path\n\nWrite p to a temporary HTML file and open it in the default browser.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.preview"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.phyloplot","page":"API","text":"phyloplot(tree_or_plot; kwargs...)\nphyloplot!(ax, tree_or_plot; kwargs...)\n\nDraw a tree figure with Makie (needs using CairoMakie, GLMakie, … — the Makie extension). The argument is a TreePlot built with treeplot and its layers, or a tree (a PhyloTree, Newick string or file), which is drawn with treeplot's defaults.\n\nThe figure is laid out exactly as for SVG and translated into Makie plots (poly, lines, text, image), so it composes with other plots: one data unit is one px of the layout, with y pointing up and the tree's root region near the origin of the layout. Use phylofigure for a ready-made Figure sized to the tree.\n\nAttributes:\n\nlinewidthscale = 1 — Makie line widths are in screen px, not data units; scale them when the axis is drawn larger or smaller than the layout.\nfontscale = 1 — multiplies label sizes (text is sized in data units).\nimages = true — draw tipimages! images (decoded with FileIO; SVG images are skipped).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.phyloplot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.phylofigure","page":"API","text":"phylofigure(p; scale=1, kwargs...) -> Makie.FigureAxisPlot\n\nA Makie Figure holding one Axis with phyloplot(p), sized so one px of the layout is scale screen px, with the axis decorations hidden and an equal aspect ratio. Other keywords go to phyloplot. Needs a Makie backend to be loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.phylofigure"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.FontMetrics","page":"API","text":"FontMetrics\n\nAdvance widths of a font (regular, bold and italic), in 1/1000 em. Make one with fontmetrics, or name a bundled one: :helvetica (also Arial), :lmroman and :lmsans (Latin Modern, LaTeX's default), :times.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.FontMetrics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.fontmetrics","page":"API","text":"fontmetrics(regular; bold=nothing, italic=nothing, bolditalic=nothing, name=…) -> FontMetrics\n\nRead advance widths from OpenType/TrueType font files (.otf, .ttf), so the layout sizes labels for that font. Missing styles are estimated from the regular widths (bold 6 % wider). Pass the result to treeplot(…; metrics=…).\n\nm = fontmetrics(\"C:/Windows/Fonts/calibri.ttf\"; bold=\"C:/Windows/Fonts/calibrib.ttf\")\np = treeplot(tree; metrics=m)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.fontmetrics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.palette","page":"API","text":"palette(name=:default) -> Vector{RGBA}\npalette(name, n)\n\nNamed categorical palettes: :default (Tableau 10), :okabeito, :wong, :tol, :set2, :dark2, :pastel (soft fills) and :bold (the matching strong hues). With n, the palette is cycled or truncated to length n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.palette"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotPhyloTree.colormap","page":"API","text":"colormap(name)\n\nA colour map: one of :viridis, :magma, :inferno, :plasma, :cividis, :turbo, :rdbu, :puor, :spectral, :coolwarm, :blues, :reds, :greens, :ylgnbu, :ylorrd, or a vector of colours to interpolate. Append _r to a name to reverse it (:viridis_r).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotPhyloTree.colormap"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Gallery","page":"Gallery","text":"Each panel uses a random dated tree with BEAST-style node annotations, made by this helper:\n\nusing PlotPhyloTree, Random\n\nfunction datedtree(n; seed=1, height=60)\n    rng = Xoshiro(seed)\n    t = randtree(n; rng, height, labels=[\"Species_$i\" for i in 1:n])\n    ladderize!(t)\n    H = treeheight(t)\n    x = rootdistance(t)\n    for i in internalnodes(t)\n        age = H - x[i]\n        w = 0.08age + 1\n        setdata!(t, i, Symbol(\"height_95%_HPD\"), [max(age - w * rand(rng), 0.0), age + w * rand(rng)])\n        setdata!(t, i, :posterior, rand(rng, [1.0, 1.0, 0.99, 0.97, 0.9, 0.8, 0.6]))\n    end\n    for i in 1:nnodes(t)\n        setdata!(t, i, :rate, exp(0.4randn(rng)))\n    end\n    return t, rng\nend\n\n# k clades, found by repeatedly splitting the largest\nfunction someclades(t, k; minsize=3)\n    cl = [rootnode(t)]\n    while length(cl) < k\n        big = argmax(i -> length(tips(t, i)), cl)\n        filter!(!=(big), cl)\n        append!(cl, childnodes(t, big))\n    end\n    rank = Dict(i => k for (k, i) in enumerate(tips(t)))\n    return sort!(filter!(i -> length(tips(t, i)) >= minsize, cl); by=i -> rank[first(tips(t, i))])\nend","title":"Gallery"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Rectangular","page":"Gallery","text":"t, _ = datedtree(30)\np = treeplot(t)\nnodelabels!(p; text=:posterior)\nscalebar!(p)\np\nTreePlot(30 tips, rectangular, 2 layers)","title":"Rectangular"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Dated-tree","page":"Gallery","text":"t, _ = datedtree(36; seed=3, height=80)\np = treeplot(t; italic=true)\ntimescale!(p; geo=:epochs, title=\"Ma\")\nnodebars!(p)\nsupport!(p; key=:posterior)\np\nTreePlot(36 tips, rectangular, 4 layers)","title":"Dated tree"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Tip-data","page":"Gallery","text":"t, rng = datedtree(28; seed=5)\nnames = tipnames(t)\np = treeplot(t; branchstyle=:round, align=true, color=:rate, colortitle=\"Rate\", colormap=:viridis)\nfor (k, i) in enumerate(someclades(t, 4))\n    highlight!(p, i; label=\"Group $k\")\nend\ntipheatmap!(p, (; mass=Dict(n => 10rand(rng) for n in names),\n                habitat=Dict(n => rand(rng, [\"forest\", \"grassland\", \"wetland\"]) for n in names)))\ntipbars!(p, Dict(n => 100rand(rng) for n in names); title=\"Range size\")\np\nTreePlot(28 tips, rectangular, 11 layers)","title":"Tip data"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Branch-styles","page":"Gallery","text":"branchstyle is :square (default), :round, :curved or :slanted.\n\nt, _ = datedtree(16; seed=2)\ntreeplot(t; branchstyle=:curved)\nTreePlot(16 tips, rectangular, 1 layers)","title":"Branch styles"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Direction","page":"Gallery","text":"direction is :right (default), :left, :down or :up.\n\nt, _ = datedtree(14; seed=4)\np = treeplot(t; direction=:down)\ntimescale!(p)\ncladelabel!(p, someclades(t, 3)[1], \"First\"; color=1)\np\nTreePlot(14 tips, rectangular, 3 layers)","title":"Direction"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Dendrogram","page":"Gallery","text":"treeplot(t; layout=:dendrogram, cladogram=true)\nTreePlot(14 tips, rectangular, 1 layers)","title":"Dendrogram"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Circular-with-rings","page":"Gallery","text":"t, rng = datedtree(70; seed=11, height=100)\nnames = tipnames(t)\ncl = someclades(t, 5; minsize=5)\np = treeplot(t; layout=:circular, openangle=20)\ntimescale!(p; geo=:periods)\nhighlight!(p, [\"Clade $k\" => c for (k, c) in enumerate(cl)])\ntipheatmap!(p, (; trait=Dict(n => rand(rng) for n in names),\n                diet=Dict(n => rand(rng, [\"herbivore\", \"carnivore\"]) for n in names)))\ntipbars!(p, Dict(n => rand(rng) for n in names); color=:gray50)\np\nTreePlot(70 tips, circular, 10 layers)","title":"Circular with rings"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Fan","page":"Gallery","text":"t, rng = datedtree(50; seed=8)\np = treeplot(t; layout=:fan, branchstyle=:curved, fontsize=8)\nhighlight!(p, Dict(\"Clade $k\" => c for (k, c) in enumerate(someclades(t, 4))); branches=true)\ntippoints!(p; color=Dict(n => rand(rng, [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]) for n in tipnames(t)), title=\"State\")\ntimescale!(p)\np\nTreePlot(50 tips, circular, 9 layers)","title":"Fan"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Clade-labels,-dark-theme","page":"Gallery","text":"t, _ = datedtree(60; seed=13)\ncl = someclades(t, 4)\np = treeplot(t; layout=:circular, theme=:dark, inner=30, rootedge=10,\n             color=:rate, colormap=:magma, colortitle=\"Rate\")\nfor (k, c) in enumerate(cl)\n    cladelabel!(p, c, \"Clade $k\"; color=k)\nend\ncladelabel!(p, cl[1], \"Superclade\"; level=2, color=:gray60)\np\nTreePlot(60 tips, circular, 5 layers)","title":"Clade labels, dark theme"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Nested-highlights","page":"Gallery","text":"t, _ = datedtree(40; seed=21)\ncl = someclades(t, 3)\nsub = [c for i in cl for c in childnodes(t, i) if length(tips(t, c)) >= 3]\np = treeplot(t; layout=:circular)\nfor (k, c) in enumerate(cl)\n    highlight!(p, c; label=\"Order $k\", level=2, alpha=0.15)\nend\nfor (k, c) in enumerate(sub)\n    highlight!(p, c; label=\"F$k\", alpha=0.25, color=k + 4)\nend\np\nTreePlot(40 tips, circular, 19 layers)","title":"Nested highlights"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Ancestral-states","page":"Gallery","text":"t, rng = datedtree(24; seed=6)\np = treeplot(t; layout=:circular)\nnodepies!(p, Dict(i => (q = rand(rng); [q, 1 - q]) for i in internalnodes(t));\n          labels=[\"state 0\", \"state 1\"])\np\nTreePlot(24 tips, circular, 2 layers)","title":"Ancestral states"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Large-tree","page":"Gallery","text":"t, _ = datedtree(400; seed=31)\ntreeplot(t; layout=:circular, tiplabels=false, color=:rate)\nTreePlot(400 tips, circular, 0 layers)","title":"Large tree"},{"category":"section","location":"makie/#Makie","page":"Makie","text":"Loading any Makie backend enables phyloplot!, which draws a TreePlot into an Axis (one data unit = one px of the layout), and phylofigure, a ready-made Figure sized to the tree.\n\nA tree beside a scatter plot of trait data, coloured by clade:\n\nusing CairoMakie, PlotPhyloTree, Random\n\nrng = Xoshiro(7)\ntree = randtree(60; rng, height=100, labels=[\"Taxon_$i\" for i in 1:60])\nladderize!(tree)\nclades = childnodes(tree, rootnode(tree))\ncolors = [\"#0072B2\", \"#D55E00\"]\n\np = treeplot(tree; layout=:circular, tiplabels=false)\nhighlight!(p, [\"Clade A\" => clades[1], \"Clade B\" => clades[2]]; colors, branches=true)\n\n# trait values for each tip, shifted by clade\ngroup = Dict(n => k for (k, c) in enumerate(clades) for n in tips(tree, c))\nx = [randn(rng) + 2group[n] for n in tips(tree)]\ny = 0.6x .+ 0.8randn(rng, length(x))\n\nfig = Figure(size=(900, 450))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1]; aspect=DataAspect())\nhidedecorations!(ax1); hidespines!(ax1)\nphyloplot!(ax1, p)\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2]; xlabel=\"trait 1\", ylabel=\"trait 2\")\nscatter!(ax2, x, y; color=[colors[group[n]] for n in tips(tree)])\nfig\nFigure()\n\nA whole figure:\n\nfor i in internalnodes(tree)\n    setdata!(tree, i, :support, rand(rng, [100, 99, 97, 92, 85, 70, 55]))\nend\nq = treeplot(tree; title=\"Rectangular\")\nnodelabels!(q)\nscalebar!(q)\nphylofigure(q)\nFigureAxisPlot()","title":"Makie"},{"category":"section","location":"typst/#CeTZ-/-Typst","page":"CeTZ / Typst","text":"Saving to .typ with standalone=false writes Typst code that draws the figure with CeTZ, to #include in a Typst document. Without standalone=false the file compiles on its own.\n\nwarning: Work in progress\nRotated tip labels and curved clade labels can come out at the wrong angle or spacing, depending on the CeTZ version. Check them in your output.\n\nusing PlotPhyloTree, Random\ntree = randtree(30; rng=Xoshiro(1), labels=[\"Species_$i\" for i in 1:30])\nladderize!(tree)\np = treeplot(tree; layout=:circular, italic=true, metrics=:lmroman)\nhighlight!(p, childnodes(tree, rootnode(tree))[1]; label=\"Clade A\")\ntimescale!(p)\nsavefig(\"tree.typ\", p; standalone=false, scale=0.8)\n\nmain.typ:\n\n#set page(width: 16cm, height: auto)\n#set text(font: \"New Computer Modern\")\n\n#figure(\n  include \"tree.typ\",\n  caption: [A phylogeny.],\n)\n\ntypst compile main.typ:\n\n(Image: CeTZ output)","title":"CeTZ / Typst"},{"category":"section","location":"typst/#Fonts","page":"CeTZ / Typst","text":"The branches, wedges, rings and symbols are drawn where the layout put them. The labels are handed to Typst as text, so they use whatever #set text(font: …) is in force where the file is included. Change the document's font and the tree's labels change with it. To use a different font for one figure, pass font (a name or a list of names) when saving.\n\nThe layout reserves room for labels before Typst sets them, so tell it which font to measure with metrics in treeplot: :lmroman (close to New Computer Modern), :lmsans, :times, :helvetica (the default), or fontmetrics with your document font's .otf/.ttf file.\n\nCurved clade labels are measured by Typst: each letter is placed along the arc using its width in the document font. Pass curvedtext=:glyphs to place each letter where the layout put it instead.","title":"Fonts"},{"category":"section","location":"typst/#Options","page":"CeTZ / Typst","text":"cetzstring returns the code as a string. The default CeTZ version needs Typst ≥ 0.13.1; for older Typst pass e.g. cetz=\"0.3.0\" (Typst ≥ 0.11). scale multiplies all lengths, font sizes included. A standalone file sets a font matching metrics.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"tikz/#TikZ-/-LaTeX","page":"TikZ / LaTeX","text":"Saving to .tikz writes a tikzpicture to \\input into a LaTeX document. Saving to .tex writes a standalone document instead.\n\nwarning: Work in progress\nRotated tip labels and curved clade labels can come out at the wrong angle or spacing. Check them in your output.\n\nusing PlotPhyloTree, Random\ntree = randtree(30; rng=Xoshiro(1), labels=[\"Species_$i\" for i in 1:30])\nladderize!(tree)\np = treeplot(tree; layout=:circular, italic=true, metrics=:lmroman)\nhighlight!(p, childnodes(tree, rootnode(tree))[1]; label=\"Clade A\")\ntimescale!(p)\nsavefig(\"tree.tikz\", p)\n\nmain.tex:\n\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{lmodern}\n\\usepackage{tikz}\n\\usetikzlibrary{decorations.text}   % curved clade labels\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\begin{figure}\n  \\centering\n  \\resizebox{0.8\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tree.tikz}}\n  \\caption{A phylogeny.}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\end{document}\n\npdflatex main.tex:\n\n(Image: TikZ output)","title":"TikZ / LaTeX"},{"category":"section","location":"tikz/#Fonts","page":"TikZ / LaTeX","text":"The branches, wedges, rings and symbols are drawn where the layout put them. The labels are handed to LaTeX as text, so they are set in the document's font. Change the document's font and the tree's labels change with it.\n\nTo set the font for the trees only:\n\nwrap the picture in a font switch: {\\sffamily\\input{tree.tikz}};\ndefine \\PlotPhyloTreeFont in the preamble to affect every tree, e.g. \\newcommand\\PlotPhyloTreeFont{\\sffamily};\nor pass LaTeX code for one figure: tikzstring(p; font=\"\\\\sffamily\").\n\nThe layout reserves room for labels before LaTeX sets them, so tell it which font to measure with metrics in treeplot: :lmroman or :lmsans for LaTeX's default Latin Modern, :times, :helvetica (the default), or fontmetrics with your document font's .otf/.ttf file. With the wrong metrics labels still line up, but rings beyond them may sit too close or too far away.\n\nCurved clade labels use TikZ's text along path, so their letters are spaced with the document font's own widths. This needs \\usetikzlibrary{decorations.text}. Pass curvedtext=:glyphs to place each letter where the layout put it instead.","title":"Fonts"},{"category":"section","location":"tikz/#Options","page":"TikZ / LaTeX","text":"tikzstring returns the code as a string. scale multiplies all lengths, font sizes included. A standalone .tex file loads a font matching metrics (TeX Gyre Heros for :helvetica, Latin Modern, or TeX Gyre Termes). Images added with tipimages! are written next to the .tikz file.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"#PlotPhyloTree.jl","page":"Home","text":"Phylogenetic tree figures in pure Julia. Build a figure from a tree, add layers (clade highlights and labels, support values, node pies, age bars, time scales, heatmap and bar rings, images, legends), then write it as SVG, PNG, PDF, TikZ or CeTZ (Typst), or draw it into a Makie figure.\n\nLayouts: rectangular (any direction), slanted, dendrogram, circular and fan. Trees are read and manipulated with PhyloIO.jl, whose API is re-exported.","title":"PlotPhyloTree.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"PlotPhyloTree\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Example","page":"Home","text":"using PlotPhyloTree, Random\ntree = randtree(40; rng=Xoshiro(2), height=50, labels=[\"Species_$i\" for i in 1:40])\nladderize!(tree)\np = treeplot(tree; layout=:circular, italic=true)\nhighlight!(p, tipnames(tree)[1:9]; label=\"Clade A\")\nhighlight!(p, tipnames(tree)[24:33]; label=\"Clade B\")\ntimescale!(p; title=\"Ma\")\np\nTreePlot(40 tips, circular, 6 layers)\n\nsavefig(\"tree.svg\", p)   # .svg, .html, .tex, .tikz, .typ\nusing Librsvg_jll        # for .png and .pdf\nsavefig(\"tree.png\", p)\n\nIn VS Code, Jupyter and Pluto a TreePlot displays inline; in a terminal REPL it opens in the browser.","title":"Example"}]
}
